WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.660
Peace be upon you, and welcome to the fourth video of Module Two of our fellowship.

2
00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:09.760
To understand one of the most critical principles in public health, let's picture a short story.

3
00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:17.000
Imagine an intense football match is underway, and three people are standing outside, dying to watch the game and see the score.

4
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.060
Unfortunately, right in front of them stands a tall wooden fence completely blocking their view.

5
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.900
Before we continue the story, let's turn to our question for today:

6
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:28.440
If we give everyone the exact same thing, have we truly achieved justice?

7
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:35.780
Suppose the three have different heights: one tall, one average, and one short. A kind passerby comes along wishing to help them...

8
00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.920
...so he brings three identical wooden crates, measuring exactly the same down to the millimeter, and gives each one crate to stand on.

9
00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.700
From the outside, you might say: 'Wow, look at that equality and fairness!'

10
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.080
Everyone received the exact same opportunity.

11
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.840
Yet was the outcome truly fair?

12
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.380
Let's see what actually happened in reality:

13
00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.360
The tall person, who could already see over the fence initially...

14
00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:56.000
...can now see the ball, the referee's corner, and the penalty kick with double clarity.

15
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.880
For the average-height person, the crate barely lifted him enough to view the match just fine.

16
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.960
As for our third, shorter friend, despite receiving the exact same crate as his companions...

17
00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.680
...the fence is still higher than him.

18
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.360
He still sees nothing but raw wood, completely shut out from the match.

19
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.280
It's as if they gave him a crate to look at, rather than stand upon!

20
00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.300
Why? Because quite simply, they did not start from the same baseline.

21
00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.140
And this brings us to the fundamental difference between two terms we constantly confuse:

22
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:23.680
Equality on one hand, and Health Equity on the other.

23
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.680
The concept of equality is simply:

24
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.000
Distributing the identical resources and services to everyone without looking at who they are...

25
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.840
...where they started from, or what their underlying circumstances are.

26
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.760
Imagine, for instance, printing a lavish health brochure written in complex, formal academic Arabic...

27
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.840
...and distributing it identically to every single resident of the city.

28
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.600
Did we achieve complete equality? Yes.

29
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.860
Yes, but is it fair? Absolutely not.

30
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.280
Because the literate, educated individual will read and benefit...

31
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.460
...while someone who cannot read or suffers from impaired vision...

32
00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.680
...the brochure ends up being merely scrap paper to wrap a falafel sandwich in.

33
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.600
He falls completely outside the circle of benefit, even though our distribution was equal.

34
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:56.800
Here comes the higher principle: Health Equity.

35
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.240
Equity doesn't mean handing everyone the identical tool.

36
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.760
Equity means providing each person precisely what they need so everyone can attain their highest possible level of health.

37
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.040
Returning to our fence metaphor:

38
00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:10.740
Equity here means giving the shortest person two crates, while taking the crate from the tall person who doesn't need it anyway.

39
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:16.620
Equity is an explicit acknowledgment that some individuals require extra support to overcome barriers and reach safety.

40
00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.520
Let's apply this to a real-world example we frequently witness:

41
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.100
Imagine a health team designed an excellent awareness campaign for heatwave prevention...

42
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:28.500
...with gorgeous videos, sleek motion graphics, publishing everything exclusively on social media.

43
00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.460
Superficially, the campaign is free and open to everyone.

44
00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.360
Yet did protection truly reach everyone?

45
00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.540
Youth equipped with smartphones and high-speed internet benefited immensely.

46
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.740
However, entire demographics completely slipped through the cracks:

47
00:02:38.740 --> 00:02:41.920
Our grandparents and elders who don't navigate modern technology...

48
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.540
...whose phones are still old keypad Nokias with flashlights...

49
00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.700
...the visually impaired...

50
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.960
...and families in refugee camps and remote villages...

51
00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:48.680
...where power cuts last for hours on end...

52
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.800
...and cell coverage doesn't even exist.

53
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.620
Crafting one generic message and broadcasting it through a single channel...

54
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.620
...has never meant you protected everyone.

55
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.340
This introduces a core public health concept:

56
00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.640
Vulnerable populations (high-risk groups).

57
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.560
When we say this, we never mean they are inherently weak—not at all.

58
00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:07.300
We mean their environmental conditions, material constraints, and physiological states...

59
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.660
...place them directly on the frontline, far more exposed to hazards than others.

60
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:14.600
Children have developing immune and organ systems...

61
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:16.500
...and lose fluids much faster than adults.

62
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.560
The elderly have a decreased physiological ability to adapt to heat...

63
00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.320
...and most manage chronic cardiac conditions or hypertension.

64
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.620
Pregnant women undergo compounded hormonal and physiological shifts...

65
00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.800
...requiring vigilant hydration and rest to protect the fetal heartbeat.

66
00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:30.820
Persons with disabilities...

67
00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.240
...face architectural and informational barriers...

68
00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.720
...hindering their access to healthcare services or even emergency alert warnings.

69
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.540
Refugees and displaced populations...

70
00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.740
...dwelling in uninsulated tents and tin-roof shelters...

71
00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:42.780
...which turn into thermal ovens under direct sunlight.

72
00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.480
Low-income households...

73
00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.420
...whose circumstances prevent them from buying protective supplies...

74
00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:47.920
...or even affording bus fare to the clinic.

75
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.080
If you want to witness health equity clearly...

76
00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:51.560
...look at scorching heatwaves:

77
00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.740
A well-off individual rests comfortably in an insulated home with central air conditioning running...

78
00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:56.560
...and a refrigerator stocked with cold water.

79
00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.340
Compare him to a construction worker or farm laborer...

80
00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:02.340
...toiling under a sun hot enough to bake stone just to put bread on the table for his kids...

81
00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.440
...or a family in an informal settlement enduring blackouts and acute water scarcity.

82
00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:07.860
The meteorological hazard is the same...

83
00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:10.700
...but the physiological toll and suffering are radically unequal.

84
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.160
What does this mean for the health educator?

85
00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.940
This places the Compass of Equity in our hands, composed of four questions for every initiative:

86
00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.160
1. Who is the actual target audience?

87
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.900
2. Who are the vulnerable groups requiring extra support and an additional crate?

88
00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:27.500
3. Is the message articulated to accommodate everyone's varying abilities?

89
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.000
4. What structural barriers might prevent people from adopting and applying this guidance?

90
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.140
To put this into practice during a dehydration prevention campaign:

91
00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.540
For youth: fast, interactive digital content on social media.

92
00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:43.140
For seniors: visiting community centers for warm, face-to-face sessions speaking their everyday language.

93
00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:49.000
For those with limited literacy: simple posters with intuitive icons and images understandable without dense text.

94
00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.380
In summary: equality gives everyone the exact same thing to clear your conscience on paper...

95
00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:59.780
...while health equity provides each person exactly what they need to ensure the most vulnerable are genuinely protected.

96
00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.680
In our next session, we will tackle a strategic inquiry that will transform how you think:

97
00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:08.440
Do we remain seated inside hospitals waiting for sick patients to treat them...

98
00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.320
...or do we journey up to the river's source to prevent them from falling in from the outset?

99
00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.820
This is what we will explore when we examine Upstream Prevention over clinical treatment.

100
00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.560
Before we close, reflect with us:

101
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.540
Pick a vulnerable group in your community, and consider:

102
00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:21.120
What is their greatest hurdle during the summer?

103
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:24.280
How does climate change compound their struggles?

104
00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:28.460
Most importantly: what extra crate can you provide to lift them up?

105
00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:31.720
Thank you for watching, and see you in the next video, God willing!
